

Thank you for your patience! I finally collected and unified all the feedback I got internally from partners 😊
We shared both with the project partners, and the feedback was very positive overall. They found the materials engaging and well-produced, and particularly appreciated the game as a tool to raise awareness of the complexity of decision-making in the livestock sector. Good job!.

Below is a summary of the comments and suggestions we got (including your AI voice question from your last email)

Video

One partner suggested replacing the AI-generated voice with a human voice recording à So yes, please go ahead with the real thing, a casting of a man’s voice would work great.
There was also a small wording suggestion for the introduction.
Current wording:
“All around Europe, ruminant animals like cows, sheep, and goats are a vital part of our food system…”
Suggested wording:
“All around Europe, ruminant animals like cows, sheep, and goats are an important part of our current food system…”

One partner also noted that the video presents a highly optimistic view of RUMIGEN's impact on the breeding sector. While this is understandable in communication materials aimed at a broad audience, they flagged it as a general observation à I see no evident action on this comment, but it is worth sharing with you.
Simulation Game

Overall, the partners really enjoyed the game and had very little critical feedback.
In the disclaimer, it was suggested to replace the word “extreme” when referring to the characters with something like “archetypes” or “stereotypes”.
One partner suggested removing the reference to “three scenarios” in the disclaimer. The reasoning is that mentioning this in advance may reduce the feeling that the player’s choices are open-ended, which is often part of the enjoyment and engagement in games.
There was also a small technical note: in “Clara’s Story”, the text on screen does not seem to fully match what Clara is saying, while the other stories appear consistent.
One partner also reflected that the consequences presented at the end of the game can appear somewhat strong relative to the complexity of global agricultural markets. However, they acknowledged that this simplification likely emphasizes trade-offs and stimulates reflection, which works well pedagogically.
Additional suggestion from my side

At the moment, the results section at the end of the game contains quite long text paragraphs, which makes the outcome feel a bit dense. It might be nice to make this part more concise and visually engaging, for example by:
breaking the text into shorter sections
using bullet points
adding icons or simple visual elements
This could make the final message easier and more enjoyable to read.

Additionally, if the decision is to keep the reference to the three scenarios, it might be helpful to attach the scenario videos presented at the beginning of the brief, as they correspond to the logic behind the three outcomes:
Scenario 1: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UogGt2VGHg8

Scenario 2: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5amUwCMGYSM

